Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and why they matter  

• `event_date` :  filter to ‟20180915”.  
• `event_name` :  find the event that represents a Quick‑Play completion.  
• `event_params` :  nested array that keeps the custom parameters we need.  
    – key = ‟board_type” → type of board that was played.  
    – key = ‟score” (or ‟final_score”, ‟points” …) → the player’s score.  
• `user_pseudo_id` :  (optional) lets you later de‑duplicate users / sessions if necessary.  

Typical conditions you will use  

• `event_date = '20180915'`  
• `event_name = 'quick_play_complete'`        -- (or whatever exact string you will discover)  
• `param.key = 'board_type'`  
• `param.key = 'score'` AND param.value.int_value IS NOT NULL  

Below are ten exploratory → analytical SQL queries (all `LIMIT 20`) that gradually lead to the answer.  
Replace the project / dataset / table with the real identifiers exactly as shown.

```sql
-- 1. List every distinct event_name that occurred on 15‑Sep‑2018
SELECT DISTINCT `event_name`
FROM   `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180915`
WHERE  `event_date` = '20180915'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look for event names that contain the words “quick” and “play”
SELECT DISTINCT `event_name`
FROM   `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180915`
WHERE  `event_date` = '20180915'
  AND  LOWER(`event_name`) LIKE '%quick%play%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the parameter keys that are sent with the suspected Quick‑Play event
SELECT DISTINCT param.key
FROM   `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180915`,
       UNNEST(`event_params`) AS param
WHERE  `event_date` = '20180915'
  AND  `event_name` = 'quick_play_complete'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Preview the raw values of board_type and score
SELECT
  param_board.value.string_value  AS board_type,
  param_score.value.int_value     AS score
FROM   `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180915`,
